  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "Why Bash Scripts Betray You",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Default Is Denial",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Bash default: keep going no matter what",
            "Failed command sets `$?`, script marches to next line",
            "Every command returns exit status 0-255",
            "Inverted logic: only 0 is truthy",
            "Pipeline status = LAST command by default"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "cd /nonexistent   # fails, prints to stderr",
            "rm -rf *          # deletes CURRENT dir",
            "grep x file | head  # grep failure hidden"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "The classic data-loss bug",
          "text": "`cd` failed but did not stop. `rm -rf *` now runs where you started, not where you meant."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Bash Is A String Language"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Everything is text — no arrays of data, only words",
            "Splitting and globbing happen AFTER expansion",
            "Hazard 1: word splitting on `IFS`",
            "Hazard 2: pathname expansion (globbing)",
            "Hazard 3: empty string collapses to nothing"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Shebangs Lie"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Shebang",
            "What runs",
            "Breaks"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`#!/bin/sh`",
              "dash on Debian",
              "arrays, `[[ ]]`, `local`"
            ],
            [
              "`#!/bin/bash`",
              "missing on NixOS",
              "whole script"
            ],
            [
              "`#!/usr/bin/env bash`",
              "first bash on PATH",
              "portable pick"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Untested Contexts"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "cron: no PATH, no TTY, no profile sourced",
            "CI: different shell, different user",
            "`sudo`: different HOME and env",
            "Docker: `sh` is dash, not bash",
            "Script bash: no aliases, no history expansion"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "Bash is right for",
                "lines": [
                  "process orchestration",
                  "pipes and redirection",
                  "file globs",
                  "gluing CLI tools"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "Switch to Python",
                "lines": [
                  "nested data structures",
                  "non-integer arithmetic",
                  "JSON parsing",
                  ">300 lines"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Trust nothing untested",
          "text": "A script that only ever ran green has never been tested — it has only been observed."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "One line, top of file, after shebang",
            "Before any logic, any sourcing, any variable",
            "Four changes: silence becomes loud failure"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Strict Mode: set -euo pipefail",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Rules Of Thumb"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Touches files, credentials, prod? strict mode + shellcheck first",
            "Test the failure path, not the happy path",
            "Run it with a missing file",
            "Run it with a full disk",
            "Run it with wrong permissions"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Canonical Header",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "#!/usr/bin/env bash",
            "set -euo pipefail",
            "IFS=$'\\n\\t'   # split on newline/tab only"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What Each Flag Does"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Flag",
            "Long name",
            "Effect"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`-e`",
              "`errexit`",
              "exit on non-zero"
            ],
            [
              "`-u`",
              "`nounset`",
              "unset var = death"
            ],
            [
              "`-o pipefail`",
              "—",
              "rightmost non-zero wins"
            ],
            [
              "`IFS=$'\\n\\t'`",
              "—",
              "no split on spaces"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`set -u`: no more silent expansion to empty string",
            "`pipefail`: `grep x f | head` stops hiding grep",
            "`IFS`: filenames with spaces survive unquoted expansion",
            "Long form self-documents: `set -o errexit -o nounset`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Tracing"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "PS4='+ ${BASH_SOURCE}:${LINENO}:${FUNCNAME[0]:-main}: '",
            "set -x        # print each command post-expansion",
            "risky_thing",
            "set +x        # off again"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "`set -E` matters",
          "text": "errtrace makes ERR traps fire inside functions, subshells and command substitutions. Without it your handler is decorative."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Version Gate"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "((BASH_VERSINFO[0] >= 4)) || {",
            "  echo 'need bash 4+' >&2; exit 1; }",
            "# macOS ships 3.2 — GPLv2 forever"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "A floor, not a ceiling",
          "text": "Strict mode catches typos and missing files. It does not catch logic errors or race conditions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Where set -e Lies To You",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Shopt Companions"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`shopt -s inherit_errexit` (4.4+): errexit into `$( )`",
            "Without it `x=$(false; echo hi)` succeeds",
            "`shopt -s failglob`: no match = error",
            "`shopt -s nullglob`: no match = expands to nothing",
            "Default: no match leaves the literal pattern"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Eight Documented Holes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "errexit is famously leaky, ~8 exceptions",
            "Every Bash veteran has been burned by one",
            "Exception 1: `if`/`while`/`until` conditions",
            "Also `&&`/`||` chains and anything after `!`",
            "Exception 2: every pipeline command except the last"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Deadliest Exception"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "if my_func; then :; fi   # -e OFF inside my_func",
            "# ...recursively, for the WHOLE body",
            "# every call it makes is unguarded too"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Last-line trap",
          "text": "`foo && bar` as the last line of a script or function makes the exit status the failing one. Silent non-zero exit."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Arithmetic Kills"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Expression",
            "Returns",
            "Why"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`((count++))`",
              "1 when 0",
              "result is 0"
            ],
            [
              "`let x=0`",
              "1",
              "same rule"
            ],
            [
              "`expr 0`",
              "1",
              "same rule"
            ],
            [
              "`((n+=1))`",
              "0 if n≠-1",
              "result non-zero"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Fix: `((count++)) || true`",
            "Or: `count=$((count+1))` — assignment always 0",
            "Bites hardest in loop counters starting at zero"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Assignment Masking"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "local x=$(failing_cmd)   # BAD: local returns 0",
            "local x; x=$(failing_cmd)  # GOOD: status kept",
            "# same for export, declare, readonly"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "$var",
              "IFS split",
              "glob expand",
              "argv"
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Quoting, Expansion, Word Splitting",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Normal Non-Zero Exits"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Command",
            "1 means",
            "2 means"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`grep`",
              "no match",
              "real error"
            ],
            [
              "`diff`",
              "files differ",
              "trouble"
            ],
            [
              "`[ ]`",
              "false",
              "syntax error"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Under `-e` an empty grep kills the script",
            "Capture-then-check: `set +e; out=$(cmd); rc=$?; set -e`",
            "Opt out visibly: `cmd || true`, never accidentally",
            "Before 4.4 `$( )` does not inherit errexit",
            "Subshells `( )` inherit `-e`, still get condition exemption"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Bottom line",
          "text": "`-e` is a safety net with holes. Use an ERR trap for reporting and explicit checks for anything that matters."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Rule One",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Quote every expansion — unquoted is the exception",
            "`\"$var\"` `\"$@\"` `\"$(cmd)\"` `\"${arr[@]}\"`",
            "Unquoted `$var`: split on IFS, then glob-expanded",
            "`my file.txt` becomes two arguments",
            "Filenames may hold spaces, newlines, quotes, dashes"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Args: @ vs *"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Form",
            "Result"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`\"$@\"`",
              "each arg separate"
            ],
            [
              "`\"$*\"`",
              "joined by IFS[0]"
            ],
            [
              "`$@`",
              "split on whitespace"
            ],
            [
              "`for f; do`",
              "same as `\"$@\"`"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Test Brackets"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`[[ ]]` does no word splitting on unquoted vars",
            "`[ $x = y ]` breaks on empty or multi-word `x`",
            "Prefer `[[ ]]`: `=~` regex, `&&` inside, no glob surprise",
            "But quote the RIGHT side of `==` or it is a pattern"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "[[ $f == *.txt ]]     # glob match",
            "[[ $f == \"*.txt\" ]]   # literal string match",
            "[[ $s =~ ^v[0-9]+$ ]] # regex, unquoted RHS"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Default-Value Expansions"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Form",
            "Fires when"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`${v:-d}`",
              "unset or empty"
            ],
            [
              "`${v-d}`",
              "unset only"
            ],
            [
              "`${v:?msg}`",
              "unset or empty: die"
            ],
            [
              "`${v:=d}`",
              "assigns d in place"
            ],
            [
              "`${v:+alt}`",
              "set and non-empty"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "`${var:?}` pairs with `-u`",
          "text": "Required config gets a named error instead of a bare 'unbound variable' at line 200."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Without `-x`, sourced definitions look undefined",
            "Gate CI at `warning`, tighten to `style` later",
            "Fragment with no shebang: `# shellcheck shell=bash`"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "ShellCheck: Your Static Analyzer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "String Ops, No Subprocess"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "${p#*/}  ${p##*/}   # trim prefix: short, long",
            "${f%.gz} ${f%%.*}   # trim suffix: short, long",
            "${s/old/new} ${s//old/new}  # replace 1, all",
            "${#s} ${s,,} ${s^^} # length, lower, upper (4+)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Kills `basename`, `dirname`, `sed`, `tr` subprocesses",
            "`$(( ))` needs no `$` on names, expands itself",
            "Never put untrusted input in `$(( ))` — it evaluates code",
            "Use `$(cmd)` not backticks: nests clean (SC2006)",
            "Only bytes banned in a filename: `/` and NUL"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What It Is",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Static analyzer for shell scripts",
            "Catches quoting bugs before runtime",
            "Flags useless subprocesses and portability breaks",
            "Four severities: error, warning, info, style"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "shellcheck script.sh",
            "shellcheck $(git ls-files '*.sh')",
            "find . -name '*.sh' -exec shellcheck {} +",
            "shellcheck -s bash fragment.inc  # shebang lies"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Useful Flags"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Flag",
            "Does"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`-x`",
              "follow `source`d files"
            ],
            [
              "`-a`",
              "include sourced issues"
            ],
            [
              "`-s bash`",
              "force dialect"
            ],
            [
              "`--severity=warning`",
              "CI gate"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Codes Worth Memorising"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Code",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "SC2086",
              "quote to stop splitting"
            ],
            [
              "SC2046",
              "unquoted `$(...)`"
            ],
            [
              "SC2164",
              "`cd` without `|| exit`"
            ],
            [
              "SC2155",
              "declare and assign apart"
            ],
            [
              "SC2115",
              "`rm -rf \"$dir/\"` empty"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "SC2086 is the single most valuable check shipped",
            "SC2006 backticks; SC2148 missing shebang",
            "SC2181 checks `$?` instead of `if cmd; then`",
            "SC2126 `grep | wc -l` instead of `grep -c`",
            "SC2115 is the guard that saves you from `rm -rf /`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Anti-pattern",
          "text": "Blanket `# shellcheck disable=all` at the top of a file is deleting the smoke detector, not fixing the fire."
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "attacker symlinks",
              "script opens",
              "writes as root",
              "/etc/passwd owned"
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Temp Files Without Tears",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Suppression, Done Right"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "# shellcheck disable=SC2086 # splitting intended",
            "cmd $args",
            "# .shellcheckrc:",
            "# disable=SC1091",
            "# severity=warning",
            "# external-sources=true"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Toolchain"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "shfmt",
              "shellcheck",
              "bats-core",
              "CI gate"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`shfmt -i 2 -ci -d` for formatting consistency",
            "shellcheck = correctness, shfmt = style",
            "`bats-core` tests actual behaviour",
            "Wire into pre-commit: `koalaman/shellcheck-precommit`",
            "Fail the build, not just your terminal"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Never Hardcode A Path",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`/tmp/myscript.tmp` is predictable — that is the bug",
            "Predictable names collide between concurrent runs",
            "Attacker pre-creates it as a symlink to `/etc/passwd`",
            "Your script, running as root, writes through it"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "mktemp Is The Answer"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "tmp=$(mktemp)          # file, mode 600",
            "tmpdir=$(mktemp -d)    # dir, mode 700",
            "trap 'rm -rf -- \"${tmpdir:?}\"' EXIT  # next line!"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Creation is atomic — no open race to win",
            "Pair with cleanup on the VERY next line",
            "`${tmpdir:?}` stops an unset var widening the `rm`",
            "Template: `mktemp -t myscript.XXXXXX`, X's required"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Portability"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Form",
            "Note"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`mktemp -t x.XXX`",
              "GNU/BSD differ"
            ],
            [
              "`mktemp \"${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/x.XXXXXX\"`",
              "portable"
            ],
            [
              "`$TMPDIR`",
              "CI, macOS, sandboxes"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`/tmp` may be tmpfs in RAM — size limited",
            "Shared between users, sticky bit set",
            "Do not stage 50 GB there"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Atomic Writes, Locks, and Trap Basics",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Skip The File Entirely"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "diff <(sort a) <(sort b)  # /dev/fd, auto-cleaned",
            "cat <<'EOF'   # quoted = no expansion",
            "literal $text",
            "EOF",
            "cmd <<< \"$var\"  # here-string beats a tiny file"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Secrets: pipe, not path",
          "text": "A file on disk can be read by whoever wins the race — or restored from the backup. Prefer a pipe or `/dev/fd`."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Atomic And Exclusive"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "cmd > \"$target.tmp\" && mv -f \"$target.tmp\" \"$target\"",
            "# same filesystem or mv is copy+delete, not atomic",
            "exec 9>/var/lock/myscript.lock",
            "flock -n 9 || exit 1   # no overlapping runs"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`umask 077` before creating sensitive files",
            "Otherwise they land world-readable",
            "`flock` gives cross-process mutual exclusion"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Shell's Finally Block",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`trap 'handler' SIGNAL` registers code for a signal",
            "`EXIT` fires on every exit path — the cleanup hook",
            "Normal end, `exit N`, or errexit death all hit it",
            "`ERR` fires on failure under errexit — reporting hook",
            "Register right after creating the resource"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Signal",
            "Source",
            "Code"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`INT`",
              "Ctrl-C",
              "130"
            ],
            [
              "`TERM`",
              "`kill`",
              "143"
            ],
            [
              "`HUP`",
              "terminal closed",
              "129"
            ],
            [
              "`KILL`",
              "`kill -9`",
              "untrappable"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "SIGKILL runs nothing",
          "text": "No trap, no cleanup, no flush. Design for being killed mid-write — that is why atomic replace exists."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Quote At Registration"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "trap 'rm -f \"$f\"' EXIT   # expands at TRAP time",
            "trap \"rm -f $f\" EXIT     # expands NOW — wrong f",
            "trap -p          # list current traps",
            "trap - EXIT      # remove one"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Traps, Cleanup, and Signals",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Reporting Handler"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "set -E   # else it never fires in functions",
            "trap 'echo \"FAIL line $LINENO: $BASH_COMMAND\" >&2' ERR"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`$LINENO`, `$BASH_COMMAND` — where and what",
            "`${BASH_SOURCE[0]}`, `${FUNCNAME[@]}` — poor man's stack trace",
            "Traps not inherited by functions/subshells without `-E`",
            "`set -T` does the same for DEBUG and RETURN"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Preserve The Exit Code"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "cleanup() {",
            "  rc=$?; rm -rf \"$tmp\"; exit $rc",
            "}",
            "trap cleanup EXIT INT TERM"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Without saving `$?` the trap's own status wins",
            "Make cleanup idempotent: `rm -f`, existence checks",
            "EXIT may run right after a partial ERR handler",
            "EXIT alone already covers most paths"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Signals And Children"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "trap 'cleanup; trap - INT; kill -INT $$' INT",
            "# re-raise so parent sees 128+signum = 130",
            "trap 'kill 0' EXIT  # kill whole process group"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Orphans are your fault",
          "text": "Background children outlive the script unless you kill the group or track PIDs and kill them by name."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Definition",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Running N times leaves the same state as running once",
            "The property that makes retries safe",
            "cron re-fires; CI reruns; humans double-click",
            "A network blip means you rerun a half-done deploy"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Declare state, not steps",
          "text": "Write 'directory exists', not 'create directory'. The second one has an opinion about history; the first does not."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Idempotent Pairs"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Not idempotent",
            "Idempotent"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`mkdir dir`",
              "`mkdir -p dir`"
            ],
            [
              "`rm file`",
              "`rm -f file`"
            ],
            [
              "`ln -s t l`",
              "`ln -sfn t l`"
            ],
            [
              "`cat a >> b`",
              "`cp a b`"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`-n` on `ln` stops nesting inside an existing link dir",
            "`install -D -m 644` makes parents and sets mode in one",
            "`cp -n` is **non-clobbering**, NOT convergent"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Idempotency: Safe To Run Twice",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Appends Duplicate"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "grep -qxF \"$line\" file || echo \"$line\" >> file",
            "# -q quiet, -x whole line, -F literal",
            "printf '%s\\n' \"$@\" > file  # owning it = truncate"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Use `>` over `>>` when you own the whole file",
            "Writing the full file is naturally idempotent",
            "Check-then-act has a TOCTOU race",
            "Prefer atomic or naturally repeatable over `if [ ! -e x ]`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Markers And Resume"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "[[ -f .step-done ]] || {",
            "  do_step && touch .step-done  # marker AFTER success",
            "}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Sentinel files record completed steps",
            "Write the marker only after the step succeeds",
            "Each step independently retryable = resumable script"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Downloads"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "curl -fsSL --retry 3 -o out.tmp \"$url\" \\",
            "  && mv out.tmp out   # never a truncated real path",
            "# -f: HTTP 404 becomes non-zero exit"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Without `-f` you happily save the 404 page as artifact",
            "Idempotent = safe to repeat; atomic = all-or-nothing",
            "You usually need both, they are not the same",
            "Ship a `--dry-run` that prints mutating commands",
            "Same principle as Ansible/Terraform: converge, report changed"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Destroyers",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "rm -rf \"$dir/\"      # $dir empty = rm -rf /",
            "rm -rf \"${dir:?}/\"  # shell refuses to expand",
            "cd \"$dir\" || exit 1 # SC2164, never bare cd"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Bare `cd` failure leaves you in the OLD directory",
            "Everything after runs in the wrong place",
            "`--` before user paths: `rm -- \"$f\"` for leading dashes"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Footgun Gallery",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Loops Over Filenames"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "for f in $(ls)   # breaks on space or newline",
            "for f in *       # glob: correct",
            "while IFS= read -r -d '' f; do",
            "  :",
            "done < <(find . -print0)   # null-safe"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`IFS=` keeps leading and trailing spaces",
            "`-r` keeps backslashes literal",
            "`-d ''` reads NUL-delimited records",
            "Bare `read line` mangles both — never correct",
            "Never parse `ls`; use globs, `find`, or `stat`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Pipe into while = subshell",
          "text": "Variables set inside vanish at the pipe's end. Use process substitution `< <(cmd)` or `shopt -s lastpipe`."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Comparison Table"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Test",
            "Kind",
            "`10` vs `9`"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`-lt -gt -eq`",
              "numeric",
              "`10 -lt 9` false"
            ],
            [
              "`< > =`",
              "lexicographic",
              "`[[ 10 < 9 ]]` true"
            ],
            [
              "`==` in `[ ]`",
              "bashism",
              "use `=`"
            ],
            [
              "`==` in `[[ ]]`",
              "fine",
              "both work"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`[ $x = y ]` with empty `x` is a syntax error",
            "`[ \"$x\" = y ]` or `[[ $x == y ]]` is safe",
            "`echo \"$var\"` breaks on leading `-` and backslashes",
            "Use `printf '%s\\n' \"$var\"` for anything untrusted"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Redirection And Syntax"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "cmd > file 2>&1   # both into file",
            "cmd 2>&1 > file   # stderr to OLD stdout (tty)",
            "sudo cmd > /root/f          # redirect runs as YOU",
            "cmd | sudo tee /root/f >/dev/null  # correct"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Production Script Checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Skeleton, Top To Bottom",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Shebang, then `set -Eeuo pipefail`",
            "Constants, then `SCRIPT_DIR`, then sourcing",
            "`usage()`, `die()`, small helpers",
            "`main()` defined last of all functions",
            "`main \"$@\"` as the final line of the file"
          ],
          "style": "number"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "#!/usr/bin/env bash",
            "set -Eeuo pipefail",
            "readonly VERSION=1.4.0",
            "main() { parse_args \"$@\"; run; }",
            "main \"$@\"   # nothing below this"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Why A main Function"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Sourcing the file ==defines== but does not run",
            "Locals stay local — no global leakage",
            "`\"$@\"` forwards every arg, quoted, intact",
            "A truncated download cannot half-execute"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Find Your Own Directory"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "SCRIPT_DIR=$(cd -- \"$(dirname -- \\",
            "  \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd)",
            "source \"$SCRIPT_DIR/lib.sh\""
          ],
          "note": "absolute path, caller-proof"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`$0` is what the caller typed, not the truth",
            "`cd` + `pwd` turns it absolute, once",
            "`--` guards a directory named like a flag",
            "Never resolve paths against the caller's `pwd`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The die Helper"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "die() { echo \"$*\" >&2; exit 2; }",
            "[[ -f $cfg ]] || die \"no $cfg\"",
            "usage() { echo \"$USAGE\" >&2; }"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Code",
            "Means",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`0`",
              "success",
              "the only truth"
            ],
            [
              "`1`",
              "expected failure",
              "no match, no change"
            ],
            [
              "`2`",
              "usage error",
              "bad flag, missing arg"
            ],
            [
              "`>128`",
              "killed by signal",
              "`130` = Ctrl-C"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Secrets Never In argv"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`ps` shows **every** argument to every user",
            "`--password=x` leaks box-wide via `/proc`",
            "Pass by env var, file, or stdin instead",
            "`read -rs` for an interactive prompt",
            "Shell history keeps it long after you forget"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Five-Line Safety Core"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "set -Eeuo pipefail",
            "IFS=$'\\n\\t'",
            "shopt -s inherit_errexit",
            "tmp=$(mktemp -d)",
            "trap 'rm -rf -- \"${tmp:?}\"' EXIT"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "shellcheck clean at `--severity=warning`",
            "Failure path tested, not just the happy one",
            "`--dry-run` prints every mutating command",
            "Idempotent: second run changes nothing"
          ],
          "style": "checkbox"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Ship it or don't",
          "text": "Five lines of safety core, one `main \"$@\"`, one `die()`, zero secrets in argv. Everything else on this page is detail."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "caption": {
    "text": "`cd /nonexistent; rm -rf *` — two commands, one deleted working directory, zero error messages. Bash's default is to keep going no matter what, and that default has cost more data than any exploit. This handbook is the full walk from `set -euo pipefail` to a shipping checklist: the eight places `set -e` lies to you, quoting rules that survive filenames with newlines, mktemp and traps that actually clean up, idempotency that makes reruns safe, and a footgun gallery of everything that has bitten the rest of us.",
